<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Quranic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Quran is the central religious text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, believed by Muslims to be the word of God revealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Uthman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> its text was standardised into a single authoritative recension. It served as the foundation of law and governance in Islamic states, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quran.jpg" alt="Quran" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birmingham_Quran_manuscript_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Muslims believe the Quran was revealed orally by God to Muhammad through the angel Gabriel over some 23 years, ending in 632 CE, the year of his death. Islamic tradition relates that the first revelation came in 610 CE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour">Cave of Hira</a>, on the Laylat al-Qadr, during one of his retreats to the mountains. According to tradition, several of Muhammad's companions served as scribes. Shortly after his death the Quran was compiled on the order of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, Abu Bakr, by companions who had written down or memorised parts of it. The caliph Uthman, who reigned from 644 to 656, established a standard version, the Uthmanic codex, which is generally considered the archetype of the Quran known today.</p><p>Some scholars argue that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> evidence, textual variants such as the Sanaa manuscript, and historical sources suggest a more gradual and complex process of compilation. Some textual critics hold that there never was a single original text, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia accounts of its origins differ. Canonical variant readings, the qirāʾāt, are transmitted orally and differ only slightly in wording.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text has 114 chapters, called sūrahs, made up of verses, called āyahs. The most popular Hafs version has 6,236 verses. The chapters are not in chronological order but roughly in order of decreasing size, and each except the ninth begins with the Bismillah, 'In the name of God'. Traditional accounts classify chapters as Meccan or Medinan, depending on whether they were revealed before or after Muhammad's migration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p>The content covers basic beliefs such as the existence of God and the resurrection, narratives of earlier prophets, ethical and legal subjects, events of Muhammad's time, charity and prayer. Its style is allusive: events are referred to but not narrated, and people and places are mentioned but rarely named. For this reason Muslims rely on tafsir, or commentary, to explain it, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a>, oral traditions believed to record the words and actions of Muhammad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In prayer the Quran is recited only in Arabic, ideally with a special prosody called tajwid, and a person who has memorised the whole text is called a hafiz. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> was widely adopted in the 19th century it was copied by hand. The earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were in Hijazi script; Kufic scripts with thicker strokes probably appeared in the ninth century. From the 11th century naskh, muhaqqaq and rayḥānī styles dominated, with the Maghribī style popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. Because copies were costly, they were often kept in mosques, frequently as a series of 30 parts, and Ottoman copyists were highly productive. Translating the text has always been considered difficult, since an Arabic word may have a range of meanings depending on context.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Minaret of Jam, about 65 metres high and decorated with brick patterns and the <b>Qur'anic</b> chapter of Maryam, was raised in the remote valley that probably held Firozkoh, most likely in the 1190s, and is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…constitutions of 1956 and 1962 were each swept away by generals, and the 1973 constitution written under Zulfikar Ali Bhutto, which declared an Islamic Republic whose laws must conform to the <b>Quran</b> and Sunnah, was suspended by Zia-ul-Haq's martial law in 1977 and restored with amendments in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In June 656 CE rebels, many of them from Egypt, broke into Uthman’s house in Medina and, according to tradition, killed him while he was reading the <b>Quran</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Basic Law adopted by royal decree in 1992 binds the king to Sharia and declares the <b>Quran</b> and the Sunnah the constitution; the king combines legislative, executive and judicial power, royal decrees form the legislation, and no political parties or national elections exist.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They accept the <b>Quran</b> as their holy text, face the Kaaba in prayer, follow the sunnah and accept the authority of the ahadith and of the four Rightly Guided caliphs.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ahmadiyya">Ahmadiyya · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Druze sacred texts also include the <b>Quran</b>; other early writings are the Rasa’il al-Hind (Epistles of India), didactic and polemic treatises, and manuscripts long lost or hidden, such as al-Munfarid bi-Dhatihi and al-Sharia al-Ruhaniyya.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
